WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.230
Choose storage according to persistence and access patterns.

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:07.924
An instance's ephemeral disk may provide fast temporary workspace but can disappear

00:00:07.924 --> 00:00:09.880
when the instance is deleted.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:14.575
Persistent block disks survive independently and are useful for reusable environments, caches,

00:00:14.575 --> 00:00:15.749
and active data.

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:20.052
Object storage supports durable datasets, model artifacts, and results at scale,

00:00:20.052 --> 00:00:23.964
but applications may need to stage files locally for performance.

00:00:23.964 --> 00:00:27.094
Model caches reduce repeated downloads and provisioning time,

00:00:27.094 --> 00:00:31.397
yet they generate storage charges and may contain multiple large versions.

00:00:31.397 --> 00:00:35.309
Define which artifacts are authoritative, how their integrity is verified,

00:00:35.309 --> 00:00:38.048
and when old cache entries are removed.

00:00:38.048 --> 00:00:39.222
Snapshots simplify recovery,

00:00:39.222 --> 00:00:43.134
but uncontrolled snapshot growth creates both cost and data-retention risk.

00:00:43.134 --> 00:00:48.220
Back up configuration and irreplaceable data, then test restoration onto a clean environment.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:52.132
Classify datasets, prompts, outputs, embeddings, and logs before uploading them.

00:00:52.132 --> 00:00:56.044
Prompts can contain credentials, source code, personal data, regulated records,

00:00:56.044 --> 00:00:57.217
or business plans.

00:00:57.217 --> 00:01:02.695
Select approved accounts and regions, restrict access, encrypt data in transit and at rest,

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:05.042
and understand who controls encryption keys.

00:01:05.042 --> 00:01:10.519
Minimize collection and avoid logging complete prompts by default when operational metrics would suffice.

00:01:10.519 --> 00:01:13.649
Define retention and deletion for every data class.

00:01:13.649 --> 00:01:17.952
Deleting a compute instance does not necessarily delete attached disks, snapshots,

00:01:17.952 --> 00:01:19.908
object versions, logs, or backups.

00:01:19.908 --> 00:01:24.994
Conversely, automatic cleanup can destroy required evidence or results if ownership is unclear.

00:01:24.994 --> 00:01:29.297
Maintain an inventory that links storage to a project and owner.

00:01:29.297 --> 00:01:33.992
Privacy-conscious cloud GPU use means knowing where data enters, where copies remain,

00:01:33.992 --> 00:01:35.557
who can access them,

00:01:35.557 --> 00:01:39.860
and how they are removed at the end of the workload.
